<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Roundtree returns thirteen days after mastectomy with level I and II axillary dissection for left breast cancer. He went home the next day. The drain was removed on the eighth day, and the wound has healed without infection or fluid. He took acetaminophen for the first ten days and needs nothing now. He has numbness under the left arm, as he was warned, and some tightness when he lifts the arm, which is improving with the exercises. He has no arm swelling. The pathology shows a 1.6 cm grade 3 invasive ductal carcinoma with clear margins, and 2 of 15 lymph nodes involved, the largest 7 mm, with no spread through the node capsule: stage IIA (pT1c pN1a). He saw medical oncology four days ago. They advised doxorubicin and cyclophosphamide every three weeks for four cycles, then weekly paclitaxel for twelve weeks, about six months in all, starting in about two weeks, then tamoxifen for at least five years, and a radiation oncology review after chemotherapy. An echocardiogram is booked this week. The germline result is not back. He feels low at times and worries about losing his hair and about work, but he is sleeping and eating. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Surgery</td><td>Mastectomy and axillary dissection thirteen days ago; drain out; wound healed</td></tr><tr><td>Pathology</td><td>pT1c pN1a, grade 3, margins clear; stage IIA</td></tr><tr><td>Arm</td><td>Numbness under the arm; tightness improving; no swelling</td></tr><tr><td>Plan from oncology</td><td>About six months of chemotherapy, then tamoxifen; radiation oncology review afterwards</td></tr><tr><td>Mood</td><td>Low at times; PHQ-9 score 6</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

